<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ivan Vazov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1850">1850</a>–<a href="/years/1921">1921</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ivan Minchov Vazov</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ivan Vazov was a Bulgarian poet, novelist and playwright, often called the Patriarch of Bulgarian literature, born in Sopot in the Rose Valley, then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. He joined the struggle for Bulgarian independence in 1874 and, after the failure of the April Uprising of 1876, fled again to Galaţi among the exiled revolutionaries, having earlier met Hristo Botev in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. His 1888 novel Under the Yoke, which depicts the Ottoman oppression of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, has been translated into over 30 languages. He served as Education and People <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> Minister from 1897 to 1899 and died in 1921.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-vazov.jpg" alt="Ivan Vazov" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rundevuk · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ivan_Vazov_Coloured_Improved.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ivan Vazov was born in Sopot in the Rose Valley, then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, and the exact date of his birth is disputed. His parents, Saba and Mincho Vazov, strongly influenced him. After primary school in Sopot his father sent him to Kalofer as an assistant teacher, and later to Naiden Gerov's school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plovdiv">Plovdiv</a>, where he made his first steps as a poet. His father then sent him to his uncle's in Oltenița in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> to learn trade, but Vazov was immersed in literature and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/braila">Brăila</a>, where he lived with exiled Bulgarian revolutionaries and met the revolutionary poet Hristo Botev.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1874 Vazov joined the struggle for independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. He returned to Sopot in 1875 and joined the local revolutionary committee. After the April Uprising of 1876 failed he fled the country again, to Galaţi, where the surviving revolutionaries were in exile, and was appointed secretary of the committee. He was probably heavily influenced by Botev, whom he regarded as the ideological leader of the movement. He published his first work, Priaporetz and Gusla, in 1876, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>'s Sorrows in 1877.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> regained its independence in 1878 as a result of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a>, and Vazov wrote the Epic of the Forgotten. He edited the political reviews Science and Dawn, was exiled once more, to Odesa, because of the persecution of the russophile faction, and later worked as a civil servant in Svishtov. He moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a> in 1889 and published the review Dennitsa. He served as Education and People Enlightenment Minister from 7 September 1897 to 30 January 1899, representing the People's Party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His 1888 novel Under the Yoke, which depicts Ottoman oppression of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, is the best-known piece of classic Bulgarian literature and has been translated into over 30 languages. His other works include the novels New Country (1894), The Empress of Kazalar (1902) and It Will Not Perish (1920), and the plays Borislav (1909) and Ivaylo (1911). He also wrote the first Bulgarian science fiction story, The Last Day of XX Century (1899), and the first Bulgarian fantasy poem, In the Kingdom of the Fairies (1884). His work reflects two epochs, the Bulgarian Renaissance and the period after liberation. He died on 22 September 1921; the Ivan Vazov National Theatre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a> is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ivan Vazov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
